  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 AM EDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\n=== Warm and Dry Through Friday ===\n\nHigh pressure is shifting to the east, which is allowing winds to \nveer this morning. By this afternoon, we will see ample mixing up to \nabout 850mb enhanced by southwesterly flow. Dry air mix down is \npossible this this afternoon, which will bring dew points into the \nlow-mid 40s over most of the area. Ensembles show about a 40-50% \nchance of below 45F dew points this afternoon. Given this set up, \nour high temperatures will likely overachieve and reach the low-mid \n80s. We will see breezy winds in the afternoon up to 20mph. Overall, \na very dry and warm day.\n\nTonight, a weakening cold front will approach the lower Ohio Valley. \nLooking to see a wind shift from the north and scattered upper sky \ncover. Though we will not see much of a change in temperatures, just \na reinforced dry airmass. \n\nBehind the front, high pressure will move into the region and \ncontinue dry and warm conditions through the end of the work week. \nOn Thursday and Friday, mixing up to 800mb will continue to bring \ndry air to the surface and slightly overachieving high temperatures. \nLooking to see highs in the upper 70s - mid 80s these days. RH, in \nthe peak of the afternoon, may dip into the 20-25% range. \n\n=== Tropical Remnants of (current) Tropical Depression Nine ===\n\nThe National Hurricane Center has identified a Tropical Depression \n(Nine) in the Gulf near the Yucatan Peninsula. This system is \nexpected to strengthen as it moves north over the next few days. At \nthis time, this system is expected to make landfall Friday and \nweaken as it moves north. On Saturday, ridging is expected to build \nover the Ohio Valley. Depending on how these two features will \ninteract will determine the overall low track as it moves into the \nTennessee and lower Ohio Valleys. Over the last several model runs, \nwe have seen more agreement on Gulf moisture surging into the lower \nOhio Valley and a slight convergence on rainfall totals. Currently, \nlooks like rain chances and wind gusts will increase from south to \nnorth late Saturday into Sunday morning and stick around through \nMonday morning. LREF clusters and NBM ensembles show between 0.5-1.5 \ninches of rain area wide, with higher totals over southern Kentucky. \nLocally higher totals up to 2.5 inches are certainly possible. \nDepending on the exact track of the low pressure center and the \nresidence time over the region will determine if we see higher \nrainfall amounts and possible severe weather. If the low pressure \ncenter tracks into central Kentucky, there will be a chance for \ntropical tornadoes, which are very weak and brief, and gusty winds \nof 30-35mph. \n\nEarly next week, troughing will move across the northern CONUS and \nwill likely sweep the remnant TC energy and push it off to the \nnortheast. High pressure will follow, bringing drier conditions.",
